\section*{Arithmetic-Formula Lower Bounds for Permanent and Determinant}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 108; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that complex exact and border determinant representations of the permanent require size of cubic order, with the stated branching-program consequences.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Over $\mathbb C$, let $\operatorname{per}_m$ be the permanent of a generic $m\times m$ matrix. Its border determinantal complexity is the least $N$ for which it lies in the coefficientwise closure of determinants of $N\times N$ matrices with affine-linear entries. Exact determinantal complexity omits the closure. The asserted estimates are
\[
 \overline{\operatorname{dc}}(\operatorname{per}_m)\ge\frac{m^3}{5\,529\,600e},\qquad
 \operatorname{dc}(\operatorname{per}_m)\ge\frac{m^3}{5\,529\,600e}\quad(m\ge1408).
\]
The bound is cubic, rather than superpolynomial.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 108; 2026-09-24), smooth initial forms are tested by ambient polars. A characteristic-two adjugate subscheme determines a matrix on a fixed curve; normalization defects and finite-subscheme counts give matrix codimension bounds that persist under coefficientwise determinant limits.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
A cubic determinant-size lower bound gives polynomial-scale progress. The problem requires ruling out every polynomial size, and a fixed cubic exponent cannot establish that; broader permanent or determinant formula lower bounds also do not follow.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 108; 2026-09-24), the documented Lean scope is cubic bounds for the permanent in both exact and border determinantal representations, together with the explicit large-size estimate and a smooth-initial-form criterion; the algebraic branching-program applications are outside the selected declarations.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{PermanentCubic.lean}, \texttt{SmoothInitialForm.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.PermanentBorder.permanent\_cubic\_lower\_bounds}, \texttt{OAI.PermanentBorder.smooth\_initial\_form\_lower\_bound}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

